  "culturalNotes": [
    {
      "id": "yon-u1-1",
      "title": "Ẹ and o: respect",
      "body": "Yoruba uses ẹ (a respectful “you”) for elders and people you don't know, and o for friends. That's why good morning to an elder is Ẹ káàárọ̀, and thank you is Ẹ ṣé."
    },
    {
      "id": "yon-u1-2",
      "title": "A greeting for everything",
      "body": "Yoruba has greetings for every time of day and almost every situation: working, eating, even coming back from a trip. Greeting elders properly is very important; boys may bow or lie down and girls may kneel."
    },
    {
      "id": "yon-u1-3",
      "title": "Tones and dots",
      "body": "Yoruba is a tonal language. The accent marks show high (á) and low (à) tones, and the dots under ẹ, ọ and ṣ change the sound. Ọkọ can mean husband, hoe, car or spear depending on the tones!"
    }
  ],
